APSI's changes to the vendored FourQlib sources.

The files are otherwise pristine upstream, so re-pinning is: copy the new sources from
FourQ_64bit_and_portable/ over this directory, then apply this patch. Reversing it reproduces the
upstream sources exactly, which is the check to run afterwards:

    git apply --check --reverse third_party/fourq/apsi-changes.patch

Eight changes, in descending order of how much they matter.

  * AMD64/fp_x64.h and ARM64/fp_arm64.h stop reading and writing field elements through a
    uint128_t pointer. A field element is an array of digit_t, so mod1271, fpadd1271, fpsub1271
    and fpneg1271 casting &a[0] to uint128_t* and assigning through it is undefined: the two
    types do not alias, and the array carries only the alignment digit_t requires. GCC 16 acts
    on the aliasing assumption and drops stores, which silently yields wrong curve points --
    OPRFTests.Hash2Curve is the known-answer test that catches it. Every GCC or Clang build
    that selects one of these two architecture headers compiles this, which is Linux and macOS
    as well as MinGW; assembly support does not replace these routines. MSVC escapes because it
    takes the intrinsic path instead, and a generic build escapes because it includes
    generic/fp.h, which is already free of the pattern. The replacement copies in and out with
    memcpy, which cost nothing in the configurations tested here: diffing the generated
    assembly for all four functions showed GCC 16 emitting an identical instruction sequence.
    The <string.h> this needs is included outside the extern "C" block, since these headers are
    included from C++ and a standard header must not be given C language linkage.

  * eccp2_core.c, generic/fp.h and AMD64/fp_x64.h drop the __inline qualifier from 31 definitions.
    Do not skip this one. Twenty of them are non-static and called from other translation units,
    and MSVC emits no external symbol for such a definition, so those callers fail to link. The
    Unix toolchains emit it either way, which is what makes the omission easy to miss: it breaks
    only the Windows build.

  * FourQ_internal.h's generic MUL128, ADD128 and ADC128 stage through a digit_t buffer rather
    than casting to one. They hand mp_mul and mp_add, which take digit_t, operands that are
    uint64_t and a generic uint128_t that is an array of uint64_t. Where digit_t is not uint64_t
    -- the 32-bit targets, which APSI selects by defining _X86_ -- casting those pointers reads
    and writes the objects through the wrong type, which is the same violation as above. The
    sizes agree either way, so the operands keep their layout: NWORDS_FIELD digits span 16
    bytes whether a digit is 64 or 32 bits. The staging buffers are the one thing that is new,
    and they are not covered by the optional TEMP_ZEROING scrubbing, which is off by default.
    Verified by building the generic implementation and
    comparing k*G against the AMD64 implementation over ten scalars, which agree exactly.

  * AMD64/fp2_1271_AVX2.S comments out its #include of consts.s. Upstream generates that file
    from consts.c as a build step; APSI compiles consts.c as an ordinary source instead, so the
    constants are linked in rather than textually included. Without this the AVX2 build fails,
    and only on a target that assembles it, which is neither Windows nor macOS.

  * Both AMD64/*.S files gain a .note.GNU-stack section. GNU ld marks the stack of the whole
    output executable when any input object lacks one, so without this an ELF link that draws
    either object out of the archive loses NX for the whole binary. The linker warns on each
    such link, and the deprecation it mentions is of the inference itself, so a future version
    stops silently guessing rather than starting to refuse. Upstream has the same omission.

  * FourQ_internal.h selects the 128-bit arithmetic by compiler rather than by operating system,
    and selects the matching digit macros off the support macro that choice sets rather than by
    restating the target matrix. Upstream pairs target with OS, so the only configurations it
    admits are GCC or Clang on Linux and MSVC on Windows, and GCC on Windows (MinGW) matches
    neither: it reaches the #error, and if that is lifted it still lands on the MSVC branch and
    fails on _umul128. The distinction the code actually needs is the compiler, since GCC and
    Clang have a native 128-bit integer on the AMD64 and ARM64 targets these branches select,
    and MSVC never does. MinGW then takes
    the same UINT128_SUPPORT path that AMD64 Linux already uses; AMD64/fp_x64.h and eccp2.c both
    switch on that macro rather than on the target, so neither sees a combination it does not
    already handle. ARM64 is left paired with Linux, because no ARM64 Windows toolchain has been
    tested here; APSI's CMake compiles ARM64 Windows as a generic build, which does not reach
    these branches at all.

  * Every #include of a header in this same directory gains an "apsi/fourq/" prefix, so that it
    resolves through the mirrored build-tree layout described in README.md. The "../" includes in
    the architecture headers already resolve through that layout and are left alone.

  * crypto_util.c reduces both x-coordinate components in decode before the sign bit is read.
    FourQlib's 32-bit implementation, which APSI does not vendor, already reduces both here. The
    only value this changes is the redundant encoding of zero, so it is a consistency change
    rather than a defect fix, and it can be dropped if upstream reworks this area.

To regenerate, diff the pinned upstream sources against this tree and keep this header.

